{% trace-scenario title="Why did retrieval miss the document?" prompt="A document exists in the corpus but never appears in top-k results. What evidence separates indexing from ranking?" outcome="Check document version, embedding model, chunk identity, filters, query vector, and retrieval scores in one trace." /%}

## At a glance

This guide is for **engineers choosing or operating vector search for AI applications**. It helps you **make retrieval behavior measurable before choosing a vendor**. It does not rank providers or claim one database fits every workload.

### The retrieval decision model

Use **Representation → Index → Filter → Rank → Verify**. A vector database is only one part of the path.

## Choose against the workload

Describe corpus size, update frequency, tenant isolation, filter needs, latency target, recovery expectations, and budget. A benchmark on one static dataset cannot answer a production question about freshness or mixed tenants.

| Decision | What to measure | Common failure |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Embedding model | Recall and semantic fit | Similar but wrong matches |
| Chunking | Context completeness | Missing qualifiers |
| Filters | Recall by tenant and date | Silent exclusion |
| Index settings | Recall versus latency | Fast irrelevant results |
| Updates | Time to searchable state | Stale answers |

## Instrument the search path

Record query ID, tenant scope, embedding version, filter expression, top-k, document IDs, scores, and index timestamp. Avoid placing entire sensitive documents in logs by default. The [RAG evaluation guide](/blog/rag-evaluation-guide) shows how to turn these fields into retrieval and answer checks.

## Test freshness and isolation

Include newly added documents, deleted documents, conflicting versions, empty results, cross-tenant identifiers, and filters at boundaries. [RAG chatbot design](/blog/rag-chatbot) depends on the retrieval layer behaving predictably when evidence is missing.

## Release checklist

- Define recall, freshness, latency, and cost targets.
- Version embeddings and document chunks.
- Test filters, tenant isolation, deletion, and updates.
- Trace query and result identifiers without leaking source content.
- Compare retrieval changes on the same evaluation set.

## Frequently asked questions

{% faq-item question="Is a vector database required for RAG?" answer="No. It is one retrieval option. Keyword, metadata, graph, or hybrid search may be better for some corpora and queries." /%}
{% faq-item question="What embedding model should I choose?" answer="Choose from measured performance on your own representative queries, including multilingual, short, long, ambiguous, and adversarial cases. Public rankings are not a substitute for task evidence." /%}
{% faq-item question="Why are vector search results wrong?" answer="Possible causes include poor chunking, embedding mismatch, stale indexes, incorrect filters, low top-k, or a generator that ignores valid context. Trace each stage before changing the model." /%}

